# ── mail-proxy configuration ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Location : ~/.config/mail-proxy/.env      (chmod 600 — contains secrets)
# Created  : mail-proxy admin auth login
#
# SECRETS ONLY — account definitions live in accounts.json.
# The login IS the email address from accounts.json. Only the password goes here.
# Each account id maps to MAIL_<ID_UPPER>_PASS.
#
# Accounts are created via `mail-proxy admin auth login` which writes BOTH
# accounts.json AND .env atomically.

# ── Account passwords (one per account defined in accounts.json) ──────────────

# [REQUIRED] Password for the default account.
# Example: MAIL_<ID>_PASS=correct-horse-battery-staple
MAIL_<ID>_PASS=

# [OPTIONAL] Connection timeout in seconds for every IMAP/SMTP call.
# Example: MAIL_TIMEOUT=15
MAIL_TIMEOUT=

# ── OAuth2 (for Google — Microsoft uses well-known client ID, no secret) ─────
#
# Google OAuth2 requires a custom client_id + client_secret from Google Cloud
# Console. Microsoft uses Thunderbird's well-known client_id (no secret needed).
#
# To set up Google OAuth2:
#   1. Go to https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   2. Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID (Desktop application)
#   3. Enable the Gmail API in your GCP project
#   4. Set the redirect URI to http://localhost:<port>/callback (auto-detected)
#   5. Paste the client_id and client_secret below

# MAIL_OAUTH2_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# MAIL_OAUTH2_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Logging: NO file, NO level env var — exactly like `tg-proxy` / `tick-proxy`.
# All logs go to stderr (systemd/journald captures them). Level is fixed in
# code: setup_logging(level="WARNING").
